From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] crash_dump: a refused detach cancels the attach's stop

Patch 0006 attaches with PTRACE_ATTACH, whose SIGSTOP is queued for the thread
and taken when the thread next runs. Patch 0009 gives the whole dump 20 s, and
the alarm handler detaches everything attached so far.

The two meet badly when the alarm fires while the attach loop is still waiting
for a thread to reach its stop -- a thread sitting in uninterruptible I/O, for
instance, which on this single core and this eMMC is the thread the limit is
there for. PTRACE_DETACH on a thread that has not stopped is refused with
ESRCH, and the tracer's exit unlinks the tracee without cancelling the SIGSTOP
already queued for it. The thread comes back from its I/O, takes the SIGSTOP,
and group-stops the whole process, with nothing left that would continue it:
no tracer, and no SIGCHLD to anyone waiting. For system_server that is the
device, because its own Watchdog is stopped with it.

SIGCONT discards every pending stop for a thread group, so one SIGCONT to the
process cancels what the refused detaches left behind. It is sent only when a
detach was refused, so a dump that finishes normally -- which resumes the
threads itself -- does not get one. kill(2) is async-signal-safe, like the
ptrace(2) the handler already calls.

--- a/debuggerd/crash_dump.cpp
+++ b/debuggerd/crash_dump.cpp
@@ -91,13 +91,25 @@
 static bool g_attach_compat = false;
 static pid_t g_compat_tids[4096];
 static size_t g_compat_count = 0;
+// The process those threads belong to, for the SIGCONT below. Named at the
+// first attach rather than guessed from the first tid.
+static pid_t g_compat_pid = 0;
+static void compat_note_process(pid_t pid) { g_compat_pid = pid; }
 
-// Async-signal-safe: only ptrace(2). A thread that is not stopped refuses the
-// detach and is left as it is; the tracer's exit unlinks it.
+// Async-signal-safe: ptrace(2) and kill(2) only.
 static void compat_detach_all() {
+  bool refused = false;
   for (size_t i = 0; i < g_compat_count; ++i) {
-    ptrace(PTRACE_DETACH, g_compat_tids[i], 0, 0);
+    if (ptrace(PTRACE_DETACH, g_compat_tids[i], 0, 0) != 0) refused = true;
   }
+  // A thread that has not yet reached the attach's stop refuses the detach --
+  // the alarm can fire while this is waiting for one that sits in
+  // uninterruptible I/O -- and unlinking the tracer does not cancel the
+  // SIGSTOP already queued for it. The process would stop for good the moment
+  // that thread came back, with nothing left to continue it; when the process
+  // is system_server, that is the device. SIGCONT discards a pending stop for
+  // the whole thread group.
+  if (refused && g_compat_pid > 0) kill(g_compat_pid, SIGCONT);
   g_compat_count = 0;
 }
 
@@ -370,6 +382,7 @@
 
   {
     ATRACE_NAME("ptrace");
+    compat_note_process(main_tid);
     // Seize the main thread.
     if (!ptrace_seize_thread(target_proc_fd, main_tid, &attach_error)) {
       LOG(FATAL) << attach_error;
